Your strongest CPU optimization opportunity is reducing userspace work per connection, not micro-optimizing Rust syntax. XDP should reject blocked traffic before socket allocation, while userspace should process aggregated state transitions rather than individual packets.

Because no profiler data is available and the real XDP path is stubbed in your current environment, these are architecture-based recommendations rather than measured bottlenecks.

Recommended architecture

``mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Ingress packets] --> B{XDP blocked-IP lookup}
    B -->|Blocked| C[XDPDROP]
    B -->|Allowed| D[Network stack]
    D --> E[Userspace observation]
    E --> F[Bounded sharded aggregation]
    F --> G[Enforcement worker]
    G --> H[Store]
    G --> I[XDP map updates]
    H --> J[TTL expiry index]
    J --> G
`

The important rule is:

> Do not send one userspace message, perform one allocation, or touch shared state once per packet or connection when the same decision can be aggregated by IP.

Optimization priorities

| Priority | Area | Expected impact | Main change |
|---|---|---:|---|
| 1 | Kernel/userspace boundary | Very high | Keep blocked-IP decisions entirely in XDP |
| 2 | Event ingestion | Very high | Aggregate by IP before enforcement |
| 3 | XDP map synchronization | High | Apply deltas instead of full blocklist syncs |
| 4 | TTL expiration | High | Use an expiry index instead of scanning all records |
| 5 | Engine channel | High | Bound, batch, coalesce, and apply backpressure |
| 6 | Shared maps | Moderate-high | Shard intentionally; avoid DashMap in single-owner paths |
| 7 | Reconciliation | Moderate | Compare compact snapshots and repair only differences |
| 8 | IPC | Low-moderate | Framed binary protocol and bounded parsing |
| 9 | Logging | Moderate at peak | No per-packet or per-connection logging |
| 10 | Compiler/runtime tuning | Low-moderate | Native CPU tuning, LTO, allocator and worker tuning |

Keep the XDP hot path minimal

For blocked IPv4 addresses, the ideal packet path is approximately:

Validate Ethernet header bounds.
Read EtherType.
Validate IPv4 header bounds.
Extract source IPv4 address.
Perform one BPF map lookup.
Return XDPDROP when present, otherwise XDPPASS.

Avoid putting TTL calculations, counters, policy evaluation, variable-length parsing, logging, or userspace notifications in this path.

The C program should resemble this shape:

`c
SEC("xdp")
int ramshieldxdp(struct xdpmd ctx)
{
    void data = (void )(long)ctx->data;
    void dataend = (void )(long)ctx->dataend;

    struct ethhdr eth = data;
    if ((void )(eth + 1) > dataend)
        return XDPPASS;

    if (eth->hproto != bpfhtons(ETHPIP))
        return XDPPASS;

    struct iphdr ip = (void )(eth + 1);
    if ((void )(ip + 1) > dataend)
        return XDPPASS;

    _u32 sourceip = ip->saddr;

    if (bpfmaplookupelem(&BLOCKEDIPS, &sourceip))
        return XDPDROP;

    return XDPPASS;
}
`

Map selection

For 10k-100k blocked IPs:

• A BPF hash map is appropriate for sparse IPv4 keys and exact lookup.
• Use an LRU hash only if automatic eviction is acceptable. It usually is not for authoritative security policy because a blocked address could be evicted.
• Use an LPM trie only if CIDR blocking is required.
• Use a per-CPU map only for counters, not for the authoritative blocklist.
• Size the map from the intended policy capacity, not the connection rate.

A blocked-IP set of 100k entries should fit comfortably in the stated RAM range, but map entry overhead must be measured on the target kernel.

Endianness

Choose one canonical representation and document it. The C program gets ip->saddr in network byte order. Avoid converting the same address repeatedly in userspace. Create a small key type that converts once at the boundary:

`rust
#[repr(transparent)]
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, Hash, PartialEq)]
pub struct XdpIpv4Key(u32);

impl From<std::net::Ipv4Addr> for XdpIpv4Key {
    fn from(ip: std::net::Ipv4Addr) -> Self {
        Self(u32::frombebytes(ip.octets()))
    }
}
`

Validate this representation with an integration test against a real loaded BPF map. Endianness bugs can pass all stub-path tests.

Apply XDP deltas, not full snapshots

syncblocklist is potentially your largest userspace CPU risk. If it walks 100k entries every second, it performs 100k comparisons or map writes per second even when nothing changed.

Separate these operations:

`rust
pub enum BlocklistDelta {
    Insert(XdpIpv4Key),
    Remove(XdpIpv4Key),
}
`

The normal path should apply only deltas:

`rust
pub trait XdpApplier: Send + Sync {
    fn applybatch(
        &self,
        inserts: &[XdpIpv4Key],
        removals: &[XdpIpv4Key],
    ) -> Result<(), XdpError>;

    fn reconcile(
        &self,
        desired: &[XdpIpv4Key],
    ) -> Result<ReconcileReport, XdpError>;
}
`

Use full reconciliation only:

• At startup
• After attaching or replacing the XDP program
• After a map operation fails
• At a low-frequency safety interval
• On an administrative request

Coalesce contradictory updates

During each one-second batch, keep the final desired operation per IP:

`rust
enum DesiredMutation {
    Present,
    Absent,
}
`

If an IP receives block, unblock, and block during the same interval, issue only one insert. This lowers channel traffic, locking, syscall count, and BPF map operations.

Use a preallocated HashMap<IpAddr, DesiredMutation> or a compact IPv4-specific map. Clear and reuse it after each flush instead of allocating a new map every second.

Avoid holding locks across BPF operations

BPF map updates are system calls and must not execute while a DashMap guard or storage lock is held.

Use this sequence:

Lock or access state briefly.
Determine the mutation.
Drop the guard.
Add the mutation to the pending batch.
Flush the batch outside all application locks.
Record success or retry state.

Make enforcement single-owner where possible

DashMap is useful for concurrent, unrelated access, but it is not automatically the cheapest structure. If one enforcement task can own mutable policy state, a normal HashMap inside that task will often use less CPU:

`rust
struct EnforcementWorker {
    records: HashMap<IpAddr, IpRecord>,
    expirations: BinaryHeap<Reverse<ExpiryEntry>>,
    pending: HashMap<IpAddr, DesiredMutation>,
}
`

Other tasks communicate with it using a bounded channel. This gives you:

• No shard locking in the main mutation path
• Deterministic mutation ordering
• Easy idempotency
• Natural batching
• Simpler reconciliation
• Fewer race conditions between store state and XDP state

Do not funnel raw packet or connection events through this worker. Funnel only aggregated decisions such as:

`rust
enum EnforcementCommand {
    Block {
        ip: IpAddr,
        expiresat: Option<Instant>,
        reason: BlockReason,
    },
    Unblock {
        ip: IpAddr,
    },
    Flush,
    Shutdown,
}
`

If multiple tasks need high-rate read access, publish a read-optimized immutable snapshot using an atomic pointer-swap mechanism, or retain a sharded map specifically for reads. Do not add this complexity until contention is measured.

Bound and coalesce the engine channel

An unbounded enforcetx can increase CPU and memory under overload. A very large backlog also makes TTL and unblock commands stale by the time they are applied.

Use a bounded Tokio MPSC channel:

`rust
let (enforcetx, enforcerx) =
    tokio::sync::mpsc::channel::<EnforcementCommand>(4096);
`

The exact capacity needs load testing. More important than the number is the overload policy.

For authoritative block/unblock commands, do not silently discard commands. Suitable strategies are:

• Apply backpressure to administrative producers.
• Aggregate repeated updates by IP before enqueueing.
• Reserve capacity for unblock and shutdown operations.
• Reject an IPC request explicitly when the queue is saturated.
• Coalesce observational events before they reach enforcement.

Drain commands in batches

Instead of awaiting exactly one item for every loop iteration, receive one item and then drain immediately available work up to a limit:

`rust
const MAXBATCH: usize = 1024;

async fn receivebatch<T>(
    rx: &mut tokio::sync::mpsc::Receiver<T>,
    output: &mut Vec<T>,
) -> bool {
    output.clear();

    let Some(first) = rx.recv().await else {
        return false;
    };

    output.push(first);

    while output.len() < MAXBATCH {
        match rx.tryrecv() {
            Ok(item) => output.push(item),
            Err(tokio::sync::mpsc::error::TryRecvError::Empty) => break,
            Err(tokio::sync::mpsc::error::TryRecvError::Disconnected) => break,
        }
    }

    true
}
`

Preallocate and reuse output:

`rust
let mut batch = Vec::withcapacity(MAXBATCH);
`

This reduces wakeups and amortizes state and map-update overhead. Do not wait to fill the batch; that would add needless latency under light load.

Separate control and bulk work

A shutdown command should not sit behind thousands of low-priority updates. Prefer a CancellationToken, watch, or dedicated control channel for shutdown rather than putting shutdown into the bulk queue.

A useful event loop is:

`rust
tokio::select! {
    biased;

     = cancellation.cancelled() => {
        flushpending().await?;
        break;
    }

     = flushinterval.tick() => {
        flushpending().await?;
        expiredueentries().await?;
    }

    command = rx.recv() => {
        match command {
            Some(command) => applytopendingstate(command)?,
            None => {
                flushpending().await?;
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
`

Use biased only when that priority is intentional. If bulk input is continuously ready, timer and shutdown behavior should be tested for starvation.

Eliminate full-map TTL scans

At 10k-100k unique IPs, scanning all records every second is avoidable. Maintain an expiry index.

Practical option: min-heap

Rust's BinaryHeap is a max-heap, so use Reverse:

`rust
use std::{
    cmp::Reverse,
    collections::BinaryHeap,
    net::IpAddr,
    time::Instant,
};

#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, Ord, PartialEq, PartialOrd)]
struct ExpiryEntry {
    expiresat: Instant,
    generation: u64,
    ip: IpAddr,
}

type ExpiryHeap = BinaryHeap<Reverse<ExpiryEntry>>;
`

When a block is created or extended:

Increment that record's generation.
Store the new expiration in the record.
Push a new heap entry.

When popping:

Read the current record.
Ignore the heap entry if its generation is stale.
Unblock only if the current expiry is due.

Stale heap entries are cheaper and simpler than searching the heap to remove old entries.

Timer wheel alternative

If TTL precision is one second and update volume is high, a timing wheel can be more CPU-efficient:

`text
slot = expiryunixsecond mod wheelsize
`

Each flush processes only the current slot. Use this only if:

• TTL bounds are known,
• one-second expiry granularity is acceptable,
• wraparound and rescheduling are handled correctly.

For up to 100k records, begin with the heap. Move to a wheel only if profiling shows heap operations matter.

Use the correct clock

Use:

• Instant for runtime scheduling and elapsed TTL.
• A wall-clock timestamp for persisted expiry and restart reconciliation.

After restart, convert a still-valid persisted deadline into a fresh monotonic deadline. Do not persist Instant.

Keep idempotency cheap

Block and unblock should determine whether the desired kernel state actually changed.

A block operation should avoid writing to the XDP map when:

• The address is already blocked.
• The existing block has equivalent policy.
• Only metadata changed and that metadata does not affect the kernel decision.

An unblock should avoid a kernel operation when:

• The address is already absent from the desired set.
• An old TTL event refers to a superseded block generation.

This suggests separating policy state from kernel synchronization state:

`rust
struct IpRecord {
    desired: BlockState,
    kernel: KernelState,
    expiresat: Option<SystemTime>,
    generation: u64,
    reason: BlockReason,
}

enum KernelState {
    Unknown,
    Present,
    Absent,
    RetryRequired,
}
`

Be careful not to make this overly stateful. The store should remain the source of desired policy; the kernel map is a derived enforcement projection.

Reconcile by difference

On restart, reconciliation is required, but it should not become a permanent high-frequency full sync.

An efficient startup procedure is:

Load active, unexpired records from storage.
Build the desired blocked-IP set once.
Read existing XDP keys once, if the API and map type permit.
Compute:
   - desired minus actual -> insert
   - actual minus desired -> remove
Apply the differences in bounded batches.
Mark kernel state as synchronized.

For IPv4-only policy, store keys as u32, not IpAddr, inside the reconciliation sets. An enum-sized IpAddr plus hash-table overhead is unnecessary when the BPF program supports only IPv4.

Preallocate based on persisted record count:

`rust
let mut desired = HashSet::withcapacity(activerecordcount);
`

Do not write every desired key unconditionally on every restart if reading the existing pinned map is reliable. However, a straightforward full rewrite may be acceptable at startup if map iteration is more complicated or less reliable. Startup CPU is less important than steady-state correctness.

Aggregate observations before policy evaluation

At 1M connections per second, any design that submits one userspace channel message per connection will become expensive, regardless of Tokio tuning.

Use per-worker or per-CPU aggregation:

`rust
struct IpCounters {
    connections: u32,
    bytes: u64,
}
`

Workers update local counters and periodically merge them. Avoid a shared atomic increment for every connection if exact global real-time counts are unnecessary.

Possible designs:

• Per-ingress-worker HashMap<u32, Counter>, swapped once per interval
• Sharded maps selected by a stable IP hash
• Per-CPU BPF counters read in batches
• Space-bounded approximate heavy-hitter detection if exact counts are not required

With 100k IPs and a 512MB lower memory bound, keep aggregation structures bounded. Decide explicitly what happens when capacity is reached:

• Evict cold entries
• Sample new entries
• Reject new tracking entries
• Use approximate counting
• Flush early

Do not allow hostile high-cardinality input to grow a userspace HashMap without a limit.

Tune Tokio for control-plane work

Tokio should run your IPC, timers, storage coordination, and enforcement control plane. It should not process every packet.

Worker count

Do not assume more Tokio workers are better. For a predominantly control-plane process, start with a modest count:

`rust
tokio::runtime::Builder::newmultithread()
    .workerthreads(workercount)
    .enableall()
    .build()?;
`

Benchmark values such as 2, 4, and the number of physical cores available to the process. Respect cgroup or VM CPU limits rather than host logical CPU count.

Too many workers can increase:

• Work stealing
• Cache misses
• Context switches
• Contention
• Memory usage

If the application performs little concurrent userspace work, even a current-thread runtime plus dedicated blocking components may be competitive. That choice must be measured under simultaneous IPC and enforcement load.

Blocking work

Aya map calls and storage operations need classification:

• If an operation is quick and nonblocking, keep it in the enforcement worker.
• If it may block unpredictably, isolate it using a dedicated thread or spawnblocking.
• Do not call spawnblocking once per IP. Send a whole batch to one blocking operation.

Spawning one task per block request is also unnecessary. Maintain long-lived workers.

Timers

Use one interval for the batch worker, not one Tokio task or timer per IP:

`rust
let mut interval = tokio::time::interval(Duration::fromsecs(1));
interval.setmissedtickbehavior(tokio::time::MissedTickBehavior::Skip);
`

Skip is normally appropriate for periodic maintenance. Burst can trigger several back-to-back flushes after scheduler delay, wasting CPU. TTL correctness should depend on deadlines, not the number of interval ticks observed.

Review the IPC server's cost model

Your newline JSON IPC server is probably not the main bottleneck unless it receives high request volume. Still, several safeguards are worthwhile.

Bound every connection

The semaphore should be acquired before expensive parsing or task creation when practical. Ensure rejected connections do not create prolonged tasks.

Use bounded framing

Set a maximum line length. Never let a client force unbounded buffer growth while waiting for a newline.

Conceptually:

`rust
const MAXFRAMEBYTES: usize = 16 * 1024;
`

If using LinesCodec, configure its maximum length. Otherwise, enforce the limit in the read loop.

Reuse buffers

Allocate one input buffer per connection and clear it after each request. Avoid String -> Value -> typed request conversions; deserialize directly into the request type.

Avoid idle polling

The idle timeout should wrap a blocking asynchronous read. It should not be implemented as a loop that repeatedly checks elapsed time.

Protocol unification

Moving to ramshield-protocol can reduce parsing CPU and remove duplicate representations, but treat this as a compatibility and correctness change first. For small, infrequent administrative messages, JSON optimization will have little effect on total CPU.

If bincode frames are introduced, use an explicit length prefix with:

• Maximum frame size
• Protocol version
• Request kind
• Validation before allocation
• Stable integer widths
• Defined endianness
• Rejection of trailing or oversized data

Do not mix newline framing and arbitrary binary payloads.

Treat DashMap as a targeted tool

If you retain DashMap:

• Pre-size it for expected cardinality.
• Avoid iteration during high-rate mutation.
• Keep guards short-lived.
• Never .await while holding a guard.
• Avoid entry closures that perform expensive work.
• Do not clone full IpRecord values when a small field is sufficient.
• Verify the chosen shard count under realistic contention.
• Avoid nested access to the same map while holding a guard.

A particularly costly pattern is:

`rust
for item in map.iter() {
    performasyncorkerneloperation(item).await;
}
`

Instead:

`rust
let snapshot: Vec<> = map
    .iter()
    .map(|entry| compactkeyordelta(entry.value()))
    .collect();

// All guards have now been dropped.
applysnapshot(snapshot).await?;
`

This snapshot allocates, so it is suitable for infrequent reconciliation, not per-second steady-state operation.

Make logging cheap under attack

At peak rates, logging can dominate userspace CPU.

Never log individually for:

• Packets
• Connections
• Repeated drops
• Every counter update
• Every idempotent block request
• Every successful BPF lookup or mutation

Instead, emit aggregated metrics once per interval:

| Metric | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Observed connections | Ingress rate |
| XDP drops | Kernel enforcement activity |
| Unique observed IPs | Cardinality pressure |
| Blocks inserted/removed | Policy churn |
| Coalesced operations | Batch effectiveness |
| Enforcement queue depth | Backpressure |
| Flush duration | Userspace health |
| XDP update failures | Kernel synchronization |
| Expired entries | TTL activity |
| Reconciliation differences | Restart consistency |

Avoid constructing expensive tracing fields unless the level is enabled:

`rust
if tracing::enabled!(tracing::Level::DEBUG) {
    tracing::debug!(
        entries = pending.len(),
        "prepared enforcement batch"
    );
}
`

Structured fields are useful, but cloning strings solely for logging is not.

Reduce representation and allocation costs

For hot internal paths:

• Prefer u32 for IPv4 keys.
• Store block reasons as a compact enum, not repeated strings.
• Use interned or shared data only if reasons can be arbitrary and repeated heavily.
• Avoid serializing records between internal modules.
• Reuse batch vectors and maps.
• Preallocate from realistic upper bounds, but do not reserve for one million entries when the expected unique-IP maximum is 100k.
• Keep IpRecord compact and inspect its actual size.

Add a test or benchmark assertion that reports important sizes:

`rust
#[test]
fn reporthottypesizes() {
    eprintln!(
        "IpRecord={} EnforcementCommand={}",
        std::mem::sizeof::<IpRecord>(),
        std::mem::sizeof::<EnforcementCommand>(),
    );
}
`

Do not enforce an exact size unless memory layout is deliberately part of the design.

BoundedVecDeque should not perform linear scans or front insertions on a high-rate path. Confirm whether it is operational history, retry storage, or ingestion buffering. For retries, a time-indexed structure may be more appropriate; for metrics history, a ring buffer is suitable.

Add explicit overload behavior

At the target rate, bounded resource behavior is part of correctness.

Document limits for:

| Resource | Required policy |
|---|---|
| IPC connections | Semaphore limit and immediate rejection/timeout |
| IPC frame size | Hard maximum |
| Enforcement queue | Bounded capacity and explicit saturation response |
| Tracked IPs | Hard cap or eviction/approximation policy |
| Pending deltas | Bounded by unique IPs and flushed early |
| Retry queue | Maximum attempts, backoff, and capacity |
| BPF map | Explicit maximum entries |
| Reconciliation batch | Fixed maximum per operation |
| Shutdown duration | Deadline with state-preserving behavior |

Use exponential backoff with jitter for repeated XDP update failures. Do not retry in a tight loop.

Build-profile improvements

For deployment builds, benchmark a profile along these lines:

`toml
[profile.release]
opt-level = 3
lto = "thin"
codegen-units = 1
panic = "abort"
strip = "symbols"
`

Tradeoffs:

• codegen-units = 1 and LTO increase build time.
• panic = "abort" removes unwinding and may conflict with your operational expectations.
• Binary stripping affects debugging.
• Full LTO may or may not outperform thin LTO enough to justify build cost.

For a fixed server fleet, test:

`text
RUSTFLAGS="-C target-cpu=native"
`

Do not distribute that binary to machines with an older CPU feature set. For heterogeneous VMs, target the oldest supported CPU or build per deployment class.

Edition 2024 itself is not a CPU optimization. Treat the migration as a language and maintenance change.

Nightly also does not inherently make execution faster. Unless a measured optimization requires nightly, stable Rust reduces toolchain risk.

Benchmark the real path

Your present evidence proves build and functional health, not performance. The stub path cannot validate XDP cost, map syscall cost, endianness, attachment, driver mode, or drop behavior.

Create two benchmark layers.

Userspace benchmarks

Measure:

Applying 100k unique blocks.
Applying 100k duplicate blocks.
Coalescing one million operations over 100k IPs.
Expiring 100k entries simultaneously and gradually.
Reconciling:
   - identical sets,
   - 1% different sets,
   - fully different sets.
IPC parsing with valid, invalid, maximum-size, and partial frames.
Queue saturation and recovery.
Shutdown while a flush is pending.

Report:

• Process CPU time
• Operations per second
• p50, p95, and p99 latency
• Allocations if measurable
• Maximum queue depth
• Resident memory
• Batch size distribution

Real XDP integration tests

Use a dedicated host or VM with compatible kernel/toolchain and test:

• Native driver mode where supported
• Generic/SKB mode as a compatibility baseline
• Blocked and allowed IPv4 traffic
• Actual XDP map contents
• Restart reconciliation
• TTL expiration and kernel removal
• Program detach/reattach behavior
• Sustained traffic with 10k and 100k keys
• Adversarial high-cardinality source addresses

Measure CPU per core, packets per second, drops, softirq load, userspace CPU, and map-update latency. Ensure traffic generation is not sharing the measured CPU resources in a way that invalidates results.

Suggested implementation sequence
Instrument first. Add aggregate counters, queue depth, flush duration, batch size, coalescing ratio, reconciliation differences, and XDP operation counts.
Split steady-state deltas from reconciliation. Ensure full syncblocklist cannot run every second.
Create one enforcement owner. Move mutable policy transitions into a long-lived worker.
Bound and batch the channel. Drain available commands and coalesce by IPv4 key.
Replace TTL scans. Add a generation-aware min-heap.
Aggregate ingress observations. Ensure userspace does not receive one event per packet or connection.
Bound all cardinality. Define overload behavior for maps, queues, IPC, and retries.
Validate on real XDP hardware. The stub path is insufficient for performance conclusions.
Tune Tokio and release settings last. Runtime knobs cannot compensate for per-connection userspace work.

Highest-value invariants to test

Add tests asserting these behavioral properties:

• Blocking an already-blocked IP performs no additional XDP insert.
• Unblocking an absent IP performs no XDP delete.
• Block then unblock in one batch produces no kernel mutation when the initial state is absent.
• Unblock then block in one batch produces one insert when the initial state is absent.
• Extending TTL does not produce another XDP insert.
• A stale expiry event cannot remove a renewed block.
• Restart reconciliation removes stale kernel keys.
• Restart reconciliation inserts missing kernel keys.
• Queue saturation never silently loses an unblock.
• Shutdown performs a bounded final flush.
• No map guard or mutex guard crosses an .await`.
• A failed XDP update marks state for reconciliation rather than producing a tight retry loop.

The likely optimal steady-state design is a minimal XDP hash lookup, a single-owner userspace policy worker, bounded coalesced delta batches, and a deadline-indexed TTL structure. That architecture should matter far more than allocator changes, nightly compiler features, or small iterator-level rewrites.
